From 5e1948b11b09e7aebe099be0397917b470f651ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:54:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] state.db: per-session change cursor (session_change
 + triggers)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Design t_01a1d563324d7e8e §2 P3, review R5 and C8; card D1 (t_01a1d59d422b413a). Blocks D2.

Why: the session list, sidebar and project tree re-rank every session on each poll, because state.db has no
sessions revision. PRAGMA data_version moves on any write (140 of 300 two-second windows on the hub), so a
reader cannot tell "nothing a listing reads moved" from "a counter ticked".

What:
- hermes_state_session_change.py: session_change(session_id PK, rev) plus a one-row
  session_change_revision(revision, epoch), fed by triggers on sessions in the writer's own transaction.
  Coverage is an EXCLUSION list (C8): every sessions column bumps except the pure counters in
  COUNTER_COLUMNS; the triggers are regenerated from PRAGMA table_info on each writer init, so a column added
  later is watched automatically. That includes last_activity_at (raised mid-turn before any message),
  started_at/ended_at/end_reason/parent_session_id/title, pinned/hidden/archived, the workspace columns,
  source and model_config (the _branched_from/_delegate_from/_reset_from lineage markers). message_count
  bumps only on a 0 <-> non-0 crossing (launch stub becoming real). INSERT, DELETE and a re-keyed id bump.
- Superseded segments: local-lineage receipts (state_meta gateway.local_policy.v1:*, the
  LOCAL_LINEAGE_PREDICATE_SQL partial index) are digested into the cursor by the reader instead of a
  state_meta trigger, because every message insert upserts state_meta (the projection generation) and a
  trigger there is compiled into each of those statements. A receipt change answers FULL.
- changed_since(conn, cursor) -> (frozenset ids | FULL, cursor'), one read snapshot. FULL unless
  (a) MAX(rev) == revision, (b) revision >= cursor's, (c) file (dev, ino), -shm identity, PRAGMA
  schema_version, the schema_version table value and the install epoch match, the installed triggers equal
  the generated ones, and the receipt digest matches. An older writer that rebuilt or copied the table
  without the triggers, a newer column, a restore (in place or file swap) and a broken invariant all read
  FULL; a (re)install rotates the epoch.
- hermes_state_schema.py: install_fail_open after the session projection install. O(1) migration (two
  empty tables, one index, five triggers, no backfill: the hub's state.db is 11.6 GB); a lock or read-only
  file never fails the open, readers answer FULL until a later init installs it.
- hermes_state_compression.py: stamp_legacy_compression_chains counted its stamps as a total_changes delta,
  which includes trigger writes; it now sums the UPDATEs' rowcounts (stamp_compression_ancestors returns
  them). The repo's other total_changes users compare for change/no-change only, which the cursor's
  triggers (fired only by a sessions write) cannot flip.
- Tests that simulate an older store with ALTER TABLE sessions DROP COLUMN now drop
  session_change_on_sessions_update first, as they already drop sessions_projection_update: SQLite refuses
  DROP COLUMN of a column a trigger names (production never drops sessions columns). The projection
  heartbeat test now accounts for the cursor's two trigger writes per stamp.

Proof:
- tests/hermes_state/test_session_change_cursor.py: 23 tests. RED with the schema hook disabled (no
  install): 22 fail (the one that passes asserts FULL). GREEN: 23 pass. Cases: mid-turn last_activity_at
  with no message, a git_repo_root-only backfill, pin/hide/workspace/archive/title/source edits, launch
  stub 0->1 (and not later messages), counter-only updates do
  not bump, a new continuation, model_config lineage marker, receipts -> FULL, re-key, delete and
  INSERT OR REPLACE, older writer -> FULL, stale trigger set -> FULL, broken invariant -> FULL, in-place
  and row-level restores -> FULL, file-replacing restore -> FULL, limit.
- Mutation check: 18 mutants of the module (each watched column excluded, each trigger disabled, each
  trust rule removed, the digest ignored, the limit ignored), all killed.
- scripts/benchmark_session_change_overhead.py (30 rounds x 150 inserts, median of paired ON/OFF ratios
  with an OFF/OFF control): append_message wall +0.99% / -0.64% (control +0.16% / -0.28%), raw-SQL wall
  +1.28% / -0.52%; limit 3%, PASS. append_message process CPU +2.3% to +4.4%: the trigger programs are
  compiled into the message_count UPDATE, which the writer re-prepares per write (its scoped authorizer
  expires statements); the commit's fsync dominates latency.
